hen able to  cross the channel successfully can we be  specific then how many vehicles  and therefore how many drivers do we  think are currently in kent  in need of these tests well as of uh the  seven at seven pm last night which the  last figures that i saw uh there were  just under 3 000  htvs parked at manston airport and there  were about  seven to 800 in other settings as part  of our operations  so operations stack uh where they're  parked on the m20  but there may be as you've you've  reported that  other hgvs and smaller vehicles are  parked elsewhere in kent because they've  chosen not to come  into the official operation so the the  true number may be slightly higher than  that  that's a sign whatever the number is  whether it's 4 000 or more  it's a significant number to work  through so the message from grant shaps  the transport secretary last night  is an important one that if you are an  hgv driver if you're thinking of  crossing  uh the short straights don't come to  kent just yet  hold off until we've worked our way  through most of that backlog  it won't speed up your journey in fact  you'll just likely get stuck in  something that's unfortunate and you'd  be better off staying where you are but  just  just on something you said there this is  gonna this process is gonna take days  isn't it to clear the backlog as you  just i think it will take a few days  to work our way through and that by  necessity means that  the delays and the issues with the  supply chain they are not coming to a  conclusion anytime soon either well they  will  ease up because i hope that hcv's will  be crossing the channel  uh this morning and so the position will  start to gradually improve  but i think it will take time we have  put all the resources as i say the armed  forces and others  are working very hard to resolve this  but just on the wider point about supply  chain  it is an issue you know i don't i don't  dispute that but  for members of the public you know it is  important to remember with respect to  food  that there is no material shortage of  food the supermarkets have all come out  to say  that their supply chains are robust if  we all behave  you know rationally if we all just shop  for what we need for christmas in the  normal way  there shouldn't be any problem  whatsoever what does the situation as  regards the testing of  of holly's drivers what does that what  effect does that have on people  wishing to travel to the continent to  france in the future  are we gonna all gonna have to take a a  test before we cross borders  well for the next uh few days  this this this uh protocol that we've  agreed  will stay in place i think until the 6th  of january will be reviewed on the 31st  of  december so for that relatively short  period you will need to  go to the border armed with a negative  lateral flow test that's been conducted  in the last 72 hours  if you want to cross whether you're an  hgv driver or somebody else  of course travel is um discouraged at  the moment so the  relatively few people will be crossing  the border they'll likely either be  people who are going  for a good reason because they're uh hgv  drivers or bus drivers trained drivers  or they'll be returning citizens but  that continent but  that that cut-off date of the sixth of  january i mean of course you know  the the european union may well decide  to to extend  the period for which they're they're  demanding this kind of testing well  we've got no reason  to believe that this this arrangement's  been put in place relatively swiftly  it'll be kept under review  of course all over europe and indeed the  world people  are now thinking carefully about this  variant and other variants  which are likely to be quite prevalent  in a number of european countries so it  may well be that over the course of  the remainder of the month a different  conclusion is reached  let's talk about community testing shall  we 17 areas have been identified as  as