ad transmission into that kind  of state of lockdown again well we moved  very rapidly for the places of your your  discussions not just london  it's london and uh you know there's the  wider south east and a number of other  places like peterborough for example  they're a little bit further afield we  moved swiftly to do that the prime  minister only received  the clear advice on friday the code  operations committee met friday and  saturday  a decision was made and announced  saturday afternoon  we've now had time to think again about  whether further action is required in  other parts of the country  and that's the decision that the prime  minister and other ministers will need  to take  uh in the next couple of days the new  variant as i say  is much less prevalent elsewhere but  there is rising evidence that you can  find it in a range of other parts of the  country and so  the question is whether we should take  action now to try to head that off yeah  but but literally now i mean we are  we're about to see people who are not in  tier four areas have  not a normal christmas by any means we  all admit that  but there will be households sharing  households mixing  indoors in the environments in which we  understand the first variant of code 19  spread most rapidly the question is why  are we not doing this now and why are we  still permitting  large parts of the country many millions  of people  to mix together well we have taken  action in the rest of the country  there are only one or two places which  are currently in tier one which is the  most  uh relaxed form of restriction vast ways  of the country are in tier three  where they are facing significant  challenges which uh were considered  uh very tough until right until the last  week when we met we've added tier four  to the mix  so it's not as if the rest of the  country is having an easy ride  all parts of the country have clear  guidelines and rules to follow  the best thing everyone can do at home  re